  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday night through Friday)\nIssued at 200 AM CST Sat Nov 7 2026\n\nLingering showers stick around portions of the Big Bend extending \ninto the Permian Basin Sunday night through Monday morning. This is \nthanks to the upper low stalling and becoming much broader near the \nregion. Locations south of I-20 will still have the greatest chance \n(30-60%) of seeing rain during this timeframe. These showers are \nexpected to remain light to moderate where the flash flooding threat \nremains low. Ponding of water in low-lying, urban, and poor drainage \nareas may occur due to any previous rainfall and already saturated\nsoils/surfaces. By Monday, much of this activity looks to \ndiminish and propagate eastward as the upper-level jet \nstrengthens across portions of South TX. Overcast skies and light \nlingering showers will create another dreary weather day across \nmuch of the region. Highs are forecast in the upper 50s to 60s for\nmost locations.\n\nThe weather pattern becomes more benign beginning on Tuesday lasting \nthrough the rest of the work week. Upper-level ridging returns, \npromoting warmer and dry conditions. As a result, near normal \nhighs for this time of year are forecast ranging from the mid 60s \nto mid 70s areawide. Lows look to remain above normal (40s to low \n50s) which will prolong the first frost date for many areas in the\nmeantime. Looking further into the extended, guidance has another\nupper-level disturbance approaching the region by next weekend, \nfavoring another potential setup for rain/storms. There is quite a\nbit of disagreement amongst models of the track, positioning, and\nstrength of the system. Therefore, details will become more \nclearer in the coming days. \n\nLamberson",
